event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"### 4. Routing: Finding the Path\nOnce your computer has the destination IP address, its generated packets are sent out into the network","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". They encounter hardware devices called **routers**. \n\nRouters sit at the intersections of different networks. Their job is to","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" examine the destination address of each incoming packet and consult a routing table to determine the most efficient next hop to get the packet closer to its destination.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Because the internet is a mesh network with multiple paths between any two points, different packets belonging to the same web page might take entirely","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" different routes. Once they all arrive at the destination, the receiving device reassembles the packets in the correct order using the sequence numbers in","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" their headers.\n\n### 5. Transport: TCP and UDP\nHow do we ensure packets arrive reliably? This is the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" job of transport protocols, most notably **TCP** (Transmission Control Protocol) and **UDP** (User Datagram Protocol","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":").\n\n*   **TCP** is meticulous and reliable. Before sending any data, TCP initiates a \"handshake\" to","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" establish a connection between your computer and the server. It numbers every packet, tracks which ones are received, and requests re","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"transmission of any lost packets. This is what you use for web browsing, emails, and file downloads, where data integrity","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" is critical.\n*   **UDP** is fast but reckless. It fires packets off to the destination without establishing a connection and","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" doesn’t check if they arrive. This is used for live streaming, online gaming, and video calls, where dropping a single","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" frame of video is preferable to pausing the stream to wait for a retransmission.\n\n### 6. The Application Layer: HTTP and","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" HTTPS\nOnce the physical connection is made, the packets are routed, and the transport protocol ensures reliable delivery, the receiving","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" server needs to know *what* to do with the data. This is where **HTTP** (Hypertext Transfer Protocol","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":") or its secure version, **HTTPS**, comes in.\n\nHTTP is a request-response protocol operating at the application layer. When","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

